<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Botswana</h1><p class="st-meta">1966 CE – present · capital Gaborone</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A landlocked southern African republic, independent from Britain since 1966, with uninterrupted elections and an economy built on diamonds.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bechuanaland-protectorate">Bechuanaland Protectorate</a> gained self-government in 1965, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaborone">Gaborone</a> became the capital. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana-democratic-party">Bechuanaland Democratic Party</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seretse-khama">Seretse Khama</a> in 1962, won the first general election. On 30 September 1966 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> became the independent Republic of Botswana, with Seretse Khama as its first president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Botswana set up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary system</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-system">Westminster model</a>, keeping chiefs and the kgotla within the national legal system. It was poor at independence, with little infrastructure and an economy that depended on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>. The discovery of diamonds in 1967 transformed it, and a partnership with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-beers">De Beers</a> through Debswana gave the government large revenues. Quett Masire became president after Khama died in 1980, and Festus Mogae followed in 1998. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiv-aids">HIV/AIDS</a> cut life expectancy from 67 to 50 years in one decade. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana-democratic-party">Botswana Democratic Party</a> governed from 1966 until 2024.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Ian Khama, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seretse-khama">Seretse Khama</a>, became president on 1 April 2008. Mokgweetsi Masisi was elected president after the 2019 general election. In the 2024 general election the Umbrella for Democratic Change defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana-democratic-party">Botswana Democratic Party</a>, ending 58 years of its rule, and Duma Boko became president.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/botswana.jpg" alt="Botswana" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: SKopp , Gabbe , Madden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Botswana.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1966 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gaborone</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Seretse Khama, Quett Masire, Festus Mogae, Ian Khama, Mokgweetsi Masisi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity (an estimated 77%), mainly Anglican, Methodist and United Congregational</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English (official), Setswana</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/botswana">Botswana</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Botswana</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1996&amp;focus=botswana" aria-label="Botswana on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5368 3085 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Botswana lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5688 3341L5688 3341"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5688 3341h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5688 3341h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5688 3341h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Gaborone. <a href="/atlas?year=1996&amp;focus=botswana">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
